        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Decide the fuel question deliberately.</strong> Full and stabilised with the treated fuel run through the carburetor, or completely dry with the bowl drained. The default — half a tank of untreated petrol for eight months — is what varnishes carburetors and causes most no-starts.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then run it monthly, under an actual load, for twenty to thirty minutes. That single habit keeps the carburetor clean, maintains the alternator's residual magnetism, and surfaces problems on a calm afternoon instead of during a storm.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Store it dry, off the floor, under a breathable cover rather than a tarp, with the battery on a maintainer and a box of cords, oil and a torch beside it. Then do one proper check each September, while parts are available and nothing is urgent.
